I came to this after the fact and had to read the encoder to follow it, so =
let me restate what I take the finding to be and you can correct me. The le=
ngth field is populated after padding, hence 52 everywhere; the supervision=
 mask is applied to the answer span before truncation is applied to the seq=
uence; truncation then removes the head of the answer but the mask that was=
 computed over the full span survives on whatever remains. If I had to gues=
s, that ordering is also why the ratio has been suspiciously flat in every =
report since we moved to the fixed-length encoder.

That said, I would put one refinement on the remedy. A truncated answer is =
not merely an unsupervised example =E2=80=94 it is an example we should be =
able to detect and count, otherwise we will lose the signal about how often=
 our sequences are too short for the material. If we drop those twelve posi=
tions from supervision and say nothing else, supervised_tokens quietly fall=
s and we will have no way to tell a genuinely short answer from one we muti=
lated. I would like a per-row marker for the truncated case so that the los=
s of supervision is visible as such.

On the wider question, I agree with Nikolai that the two changes belong tog=
ether.

Dermot
